{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r5.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"point\": 1,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"tight market conditions could last until 2028\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron (memory supplier)\",\n    \"what\": \"memory market stays tight: demand above supply, prices sharply higher, new industry capacity not online until 2027 or later\",\n    \"period\": \"until 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided to $50 billion of revenue next quarter, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying Nvidia is expected to generate roughly $100 billion in a comparable period. The Nvidia figure is an implied comparison, not a stated figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced that tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecasts that new capacity in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half from mid-2026, which could lead to even higher memory chip prices because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 2,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n   \"passage\": \"new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n    \"what\": \"new memory capacity comes online only in 2027 or later, so memory supply stays low\",\n    \"period\": \"at least the next year and a half\",\n    \"status\": \"view with a reason\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided to $50 billion of revenue next quarter, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying Nvidia is expected to generate roughly $100 billion in a comparable period. The Nvidia figure is an implied comparison, not a stated figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced that tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecasts that new capacity in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half from mid-2026, which could lead to even higher memory chip prices because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 3,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron\",\n    \"what\": \"revenue guidance\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$50bn\",\n    \"period\": \"Micron's next fiscal quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury writes that the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year (calendar 2026), and that next year (calendar 2027) that figure could be over $1 trillion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology guided to $50 billion of revenue next quarter, which Fool.com's Keithen Drury says is about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate, implying Nvidia is expected to generate roughly $100 billion in a comparable period. The Nvidia figure is an implied comparison, not a stated figure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology announced that tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028, according to Fool.com.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Fool.com's Keithen Drury forecasts that new capacity in the memory industry will not be online until 2027 or later, leaving low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half from mid-2026, which could lead to even higher memory chip prices because demand is not slowing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"As mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 4,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as cited)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron guidance\",\n    \"what\": \"adjusted gross margin (memory pricing power)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"86%\",\n    \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion in the quarter the article cites, with data center revenue up 92% to $75.2 billion; the article uses this to argue the reported earnings do not explain the stock's 2026 underperformance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA carries $119 billion in supply commitments, which the article says hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, a question it says the market is already pricing in.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA shares were reported to be under pressure on reports of delays across its upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems; the reports are unattributed in the article.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA made a $5 billion equity investment in Intel, cited by the article as one driver of Intel's 278.4% year-to-date share move.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided its fiscal Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year, a rival's guidance in the AI accelerator/custom-silicon market NVIDIA serves.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts a 27% compound annual growth rate for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators, as Broadcom's custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta erode NVIDIA's narrative despite NVIDIA still holding roughly 81% of the AI chip market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"AMD's Meta Instinct GPU deal underpins its multi-year hyperscaler position, per the article — a named hyperscaler customer running a rival's AI accelerators.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"AMD CEO Lisa Su said customer forecasts for the MI450 GPU are exceeding the company's initial expectations, a rival's demand claim in NVIDIA's AI accelerator market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Technology's Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointing to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability — a key memory supplier's reported result and contracted demand.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron is a different animal.\",\n     \"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra pointed to multi-year Strategic Customer Agreements designed to lock in durability.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e10\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron is forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% for its next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA has ever achieved, per the article.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Most strikingly, Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter, above the peak gross margins NVIDIA ever achieved.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e11\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027, per the article, as memory's share of the data center trade rises relative to NVIDIA's accelerators.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 5,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information, citing two unidentified people (via Bloomberg / Straits Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Chinese officials\",\n    \"what\": \"permission for top AI companies (Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek) to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, each on approval stating the number and reason\",\n    \"how_much\": \"a limited amount; total may be fewer than 200,000\",\n    \"condition\": \"approval per request; limited amount\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on 8 July 2026, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter, that Chinese officials have told Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek they will have permission to buy limited quantities of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing prior restrictions. Buyers must request approval specifying how many chips they need and why, and the Chinese government is still deciding the total allowed, which may end up fewer than 200,000 chips.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\",\n     \"Chinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\",\n     \"Chinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\",\n     \"The Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia is working to release its next-generation Rubin AI chip line-up in the second half of 2026, following the Blackwell generation introduced in late 2024, according to the article.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission in December to sell the processors (H200 AI chips) to China, but Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country, the article reports.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Though US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China, and that Nvidia was uncertain whether any imports would be allowed into the country.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\",\n     \"\\\"We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,\\\" she said.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 6,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"China's government (stated by Tom's Hardware)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Beijing\",\n    \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\",\n     \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 7,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"an unnamed tech executive (to the Financial Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\",\n    \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\",\n     \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 8,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"US President (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"US President Trump\",\n    \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\",\n    \"period\": \"December 2025\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\",\n     \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\",\n     \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 9,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Sharon AI (counterparty disclosure, reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\",\n    \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\",\n    \"period\": \"six years\",\n    \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 10,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n    \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n    \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 11,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n    \"period\": \"over six years\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 12,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 13,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n    \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n    \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n    \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on July 1 that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement; an NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the program's existence.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n     \"According to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per the official press release, NVIDIA will earn standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream; the company positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model. Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in the model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\",\n     \"NVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\",\n     \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"As the first participants in NVIDIA's model, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May 2026 (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 14,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (post on Monday)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n    \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n    \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n    \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 15,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n    \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\",\n     \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\",\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 16,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n    \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n    \"status\": \"plan\",\n    \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\",\n     \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\",\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 17,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (statement to CNBC)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\",\n     \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\",\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\",\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\",\n     \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\",\n     \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\",\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 18,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n   \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n    \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n    \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev1\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to SemiAnalysis, in a thread on X reported by Tom's Hardware, NVIDIA will not ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028 — a delay of more than 12 months — pushing the cabinet intended for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year. The report attributes the holdup to manufacturing challenges with the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA calls the orthogonal backplane) that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backplane.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev2\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack that would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. The report says no proven alternative remains to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\",\n     \"NVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev3\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVL576 — a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics — is likely to slip too, or to ship in low volume, until that optical technology matures.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev4\",\n    \"summary\": \"An NVIDIA representative told Tom's Hardware, in a statement dated 7/6/2026, that \\\"Our roadmap is intact,\\\" providing no further details in response to follow-up questions about the reported Kyber delay.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Update 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to the report, NVIDIA dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part about a week before the 7/6/2026 article, over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Tom's Hardware reported that the delay applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack; NVIDIA's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, are not part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 19,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (May 2026 quarterly filing)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n    \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n    \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on July 1 that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement; an NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the program's existence.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n     \"According to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per the official press release, NVIDIA will earn standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream; the company positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model. Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in the model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n     \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\",\n     \"NVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\",\n     \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"As the first participants in NVIDIA's model, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May 2026 (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 20,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r5.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p[5:17]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r5.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in p[5:17]]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"point\": 6, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"China's government (stated by Tom's Hardware)\", \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Beijing\", \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\", \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\", \"passages\": [\"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\", \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 7, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"an unnamed tech executive (to the Financial Times)\", \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\", \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\", \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\", \"passages\": [\"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\", \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 8, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"who\": \"US President (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"US President Trump\", \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\", \"period\": \"December 2025\", \"status\": \"happened\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e1\", \"summary\": \"Nvidia has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to stop its AI chips and servers being smuggled into China, cutting the number of authorized clients by more than half, according to the Financial Times; remaining clients passed tougher compliance inspections to confirm they are genuine businesses rather than shell companies. Nvidia's reported steps included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification and interviewing end users. Sources told the FT Nvidia made the move after Washington pressured it to tighten its legal compliance.\", \"passages\": [\"AI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\", \"Sources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\", \"summary\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia's H200 AI processors — which the U.S. had permitted for export to select customers in the region — with China instead banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, which the article says is apparently still not enough. The block was in force at the time of the 2026-07-14 report, though no decision date is given.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it's banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up the shortfall, but it's apparently still not enough.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e3\", \"summary\": \"President Donald Trump reversed course in December 2025 and allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, per the article; it says this would have alleviated the Chinese supply situation had Beijing not blocked Chinese companies from buying the processors.\", \"passages\": [\"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\", \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that all domestic (Chinese) AI chip suppliers are sold out, and that buyers are even considering less powerful chips as long as they can be put to use.\", \"passages\": [\"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they're even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e5\", \"summary\": \"According to the Financial Times, Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw was arrested alongside two other suspects for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China; Singapore seized a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers, and Taiwan raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip-smuggling probe.\", \"passages\": [\"which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China.\", \"This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 9, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and D