SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\", \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\", \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\", \"passages\": [\"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 10, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\", \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\", \"how_much\": \"360MW\", \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\", \"status\": \"happened\", \"status_note\": \"announced\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\", \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\", \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\", \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\", \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\", \"passages\": [\"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 11, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"Firmus\", \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"Firmus\", \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\", \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\", \"period\": \"over six years\", \"status\": \"forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\", \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\", \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\", \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\", \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\", \"passages\": [\"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 12, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\", \"passages\": [\"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\", \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\", \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\", \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\", \"passages\": [\"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\", \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\", \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\", \"passages\": [\"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\", \"passages\": [\"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\", \"passages\": [\"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\", \"passages\": [\"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 13, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\", \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\", \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\", \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\", \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\", \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\", \"status\": \"rumour\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e1\", \"summary\": \"The Information reported on July 1 that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement; an NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the program's existence.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\", \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\", \"According to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\", \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per the official press release, NVIDIA will earn standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream; the company positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model. Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in the model.\", \"passages\": [\"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\", \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\", \"NVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\", \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\", \"summary\": \"As the first participants in NVIDIA's model, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\", \"passages\": [\"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\", \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\", \"passages\": [\"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\", \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May 2026 (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\", \"passages\": [\"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 14, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (post on Monday)\", \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\", \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\", \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\", \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\", \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"passages\": [\"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\", \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\", \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 15, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\", \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\", \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\", \"status\": \"claim\", \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\", \"passages\": [\"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\", \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\", \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"passages\": [\"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 16, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\", \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\", \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\", \"status\": \"plan\", \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-1\", \"summary\": \"Analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems, specifically the PCB midplane — a central circuit board connecting all the individual components that has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\", \"passages\": [\"Asian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\", \"The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported additional setbacks to Nvidia's roadmap: a planned alternative design, NVL72x2 — which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back — has been scrapped entirely after cloud providers and large data-center operators pushed back on its unusual form factor and high operational overhead; and the more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has been canceled, leaving only the smaller two-die version that delivers roughly half the real-world compute performance.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead.\", \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that Nvidia's key interconnect technology CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, will not arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman, meaning Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now; the report says the gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in, and Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\", \"passages\": [\"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"]}]}\n{\"point\": 17, \"decisive_item\": {\"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"who\": \"NVIDIA (statement to CNBC)\", \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\", \"meaning\": {\"who\": \"NVIDIA\", \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\", \"status\": \"claim\"}}, \"kept_from_that_article\": [{\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e1\", \"summary\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 rack-scale architecture — the server cabinet designed to pack 144 GPUs and slated to debut with NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin Ultra system in 2027 — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, because the multi-layer PCB midplane at the heart of the system 'remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.' SemiAnalysis also said the larger NVL576 system, which links eight racks via optical connections, is likely delayed or limited to small volumes.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\", \"The setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\", \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\", \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\", \"The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e2\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's backup plan — bolting two of its current-generation racks together to achieve similar power — has been scrapped after cloud service providers and hyperscalers pushed back on the design's 'odd design and heavy operational burden.' SemiAnalysis said this leaves NVIDIA with 'no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,' which it predicted could give AMD and Google a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\", \"passages\": [\"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate.\", \"\\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\", \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e3\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected the SemiAnalysis report that its Kyber/Rubin Ultra roadmap had slipped, stating 'Our roadmap is intact.'\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e4\", \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud, according to CNBC.\", \"passages\": [\"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"]}, {\"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e5\", \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projects that NVIDIA's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\", \"passages\": [\"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"]}]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r5.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)) for x in p[9:13]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r5.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps({\\\"point\\\":x[\\\"point\\\"],\\\"decisive\\\":x[\\\"decisive_item\\\"],\\\"kept\\\":x[\\\"kept_from_that_article\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1)) for x in p[9:13]]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"point\": 10,\n \"decisive\": {\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n  \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n  \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n  \"meaning\": {\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n   \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n   \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n   \"status\": \"happened\",\n   \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n  }\n },\n \"kept\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n    \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"point\": 11,\n \"decisive\": {\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n  \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n  \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n  \"meaning\": {\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n   \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n   \"period\": \"over six years\",\n   \"status\": \"forecast\"\n  }\n },\n \"kept\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n    \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"point\": 12,\n \"decisive\": {\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n  \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n  \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n  \"meaning\": {\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n   \"status\": \"claim\",\n   \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n  }\n },\n \"kept\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has stepped in and begun providing take-or-pay minimum revenue guarantees (\\\"backstops\\\") to Neoclouds for GPU capacity, typically on six-year terms, under which NVIDIA stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels and shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; it says NVIDIA has also started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\",\n    \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n    \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"In June 2026 SharonAI announced a 72MW AI factory in Australia, scaling to as many as 40,000 GB300 GPUs under a six-year NVIDIA backstop, and disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six years); SharonAI's footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total by mid-2027.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus announced on June 29, 2026 a 360MW AI cluster in Batam, Indonesia that is the next major project announced with an NVIDIA backstop, likely to be housed in a DayOne facility in the Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP).\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"Firmus said it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over six years from the NVIDIA-backstopped Batam cluster; SemiAnalysis expects a portion of the revenue above the backstop level to be shared with NVIDIA.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that AI debt financing outstanding will exceed $7T by 2029, driven by AI IT capex and AI datacenter capex needs of Neoclouds, datacenter builders and hyperscalers, making it the second-largest asset-backed debt market after the roughly $13T US mortgage-backed market.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"AI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e6\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis forecasts that annual AI capex – including GPUs, networking, storage, attached CPU compute and the datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028, with cumulative AI capex from 2024 to 2029 reaching about $11.1T, and that credit markets will be the main funding source.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Annual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e7\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says that even three years into the AI buildout, the general market's estimates for NVIDIA's shipment volumes and revenue in the second half of this year remain materially below its own supply-chain tracking in its Accelerator Model.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e8\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that only a few Neoclouds still offer one-year GPU rentals and are setting aggressive rental terms, sometimes requiring prepayments of up to 100% of total contract value, reflecting strong demand for their GPUs.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e9\",\n   \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis notes the gap between CoreWeave's 5-year unsecured bonds yielding about 10% and the 5.9% CoreWeave paid on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5B DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backstopped by Meta, roughly 90 basis points wider than Meta's ~5.0% 5-year bond yield, which it reads as the market pricing CoreWeave's project execution risk at ~90bps.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n{\n \"point\": 13,\n \"decisive\": {\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n  \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\",\n  \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n  \"meaning\": {\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n   \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n   \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n   \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n   \"status\": \"rumour\"\n  }\n },\n \"kept\": [\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e1\",\n   \"summary\": \"The Information reported on July 1 that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs: if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers' revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement; an NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the program's existence.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\",\n    \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\",\n    \"According to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\",\n   \"summary\": \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers and corporate clients. Per the official press release, NVIDIA will earn standard product revenue plus an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based recurring revenue stream; the company positions the framework as its 'DSX AI Factory' model. Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in the model.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\",\n    \"According to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream.\",\n    \"NVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\",\n    \"Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\",\n   \"summary\": \"As the first participants in NVIDIA's model, Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\n   \"summary\": \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\n   \"summary\": \"According to a regulatory filing NVIDIA submitted in May 2026 (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\",\n   \"passages\": [\n    \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"point\":1,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-3\",\"why\":\"Micron said tight memory-market conditions could last until 2028.\"}\n{\"point\":2,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-4\",\"why\":\"New memory capacity is not expected online until 2027 or later, leaving supply low for at least a year and a half.\"}\n{\"point\":3,\"extracted\":\"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#ev-2\",\"why\":\"Micron guided to $50 billion in revenue next quarter.\"}\n{\"point\":4,\"extracted\":\"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e10\",\"why\":\"Micron forecast an 86% adjusted gross margin next quarter.\"}\n{\"point\":5,\"extracted\":\"gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c#e1\",\"why\":\"Chinese officials reportedly planned to allow limited H200 purchases subject to approval, potentially totaling fewer than 200,000 chips.\"}\n{\"point\":6,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\"why\":\"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy Nvidia H200 processors.\"}\n{\"point\":7,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e4\",\"why\":\"An executive said Chinese AI chip suppliers were sold out and buyers were considering less powerful chips.\"}\n{\"point\":8,\"extracted\":\"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e2\",\"why\":\"The quoted passage says Trump allowed H200 exports to select customers in December 2025.\"}\n{\"point\":9,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e2\",\"why\":\"Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion backstop for its Australian project.\"}\n{\"point\":10,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e3\",\"why\":\"Firmus announced a 360 MW cluster in Batam with an Nvidia backstop.\"}\n{\"point\":11,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e4\",\"why\":\"Firmus expected $25 billion to $30 billion in customer revenue over six years.\"}\n{\"point\":12,\"extracted\":\"none\",\"why\":\"No kept item states that undisclosed backstops exist, many neoclouds are negotiating, or announcements are accelerating.\"}\n{\"point\":13,\"extracted\":\"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\"why\":\"Nvidia was reportedly negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's Ohio data center lease.\"}\n{\"point\":14,\"extracted\":\"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e1\",\"why\":\"SemiAnalysis reported Kyber NVL144 delayed to 2028 because the PCB midplane was difficult to manufacture.\"}\n{\"point\":15,\"extracted\":\"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-2\",\"why\":\"The four-die Rubin Ultra was reportedly canceled, leaving a two-die version with roughly half the compute performance.\"}\n{\"point\":16,\"extracted\":\"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#ev-3\",\"why\":\"Nvidia reportedly planned to sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the shortfall.\"}\n{\"point\":17,\"extracted\":\"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e3\",\"why\":\"Nvidia rejected the delay report and said its roadmap was intact.\"}\n{\"point\":18,\"extracted\":\"gnews:c75f16a6d64cc27fc28fb571#ev4\",\"why\":\"An Nvidia representative said the roadmap was intact.\"}\n{\"point\":19,\"extracted\":\"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\"why\":\"Nvidia added $3.5 billion in customer data center lease guarantees in exchange for rights to buy shares.\"}\n{\"point\":20,\"extracted\":\"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e1\",\"why\":\"SemiAnalysis said Nvidia had started backstopping data center leases.\"}"}}
